WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:03.191
<v Examiner>Exam case 4, continued.

2
00:00:03.191 --> 00:00:04.973
<v Examiner>Breathing and blood pH.

3
00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:09.343
One idea runs through this whole track, so get it first.

4
00:00:09.343 --> 00:00:12.634
Carbon dioxide and hydrogen ions move together.

5
00:00:12.634 --> 00:00:15.785
Whatever happens to one happens to the other.

6
00:00:15.785 --> 00:00:18.585
And more hydrogen ions means a lower pH.

7
00:00:19.035 --> 00:00:19.675
<v Examiner>One mark.

8
00:00:19.675 --> 00:00:21.382
<v Examiner>What is normal blood pH?

9
00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:27.322
Seven point three five to seven point four five, averaging seven point four.

10
00:00:27.322 --> 00:00:30.284
Below seven point three five is acidotic.

11
00:00:30.284 --> 00:00:33.245
Above seven point four five is alkalotic.

12
00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:34.248
<v Examiner>One mark.

13
00:00:34.248 --> 00:00:37.506
<v Examiner>Write the chemical equation, in words or in formulas,

14
00:00:37.506 --> 00:00:40.394
<v Examiner>for the bicarbonate buffer system in the blood.

15
00:00:40.844 --> 00:00:45.542
Carbon dioxide plus water, in balance with carbonic acid,

16
00:00:45.542 --> 00:00:49.827
in balance with hydrogen ions plus bicarbonate ions.

17
00:00:49.827 --> 00:00:56.008
In formulas: C O 2 plus H 2 O, to H 2 C O 3, to H plus, plus H C O 3 minus.

18
00:00:56.008 --> 00:00:58.316
And the arrows go both ways.

19
00:00:59.766 --> 00:01:00.573
<v Examiner>Three marks.

20
00:01:00.573 --> 00:01:05.952
<v Examiner>How do changes in the respiratory rate and depth, like in Manaia's panic attack,

21
00:01:05.952 --> 00:01:07.297
<v Examiner>affect the blood pH?

22
00:01:07.747 --> 00:01:13.153
The hook: blow off carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions fall, pH rises.

23
00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:13.997
Alkalosis.

24
00:01:14.447 --> 00:01:20.851
Changing the rate and depth of breathing changes the carbon dioxide level in the alveoli,

25
00:01:20.851 --> 00:01:22.290
and so in the blood.

26
00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:27.255
Blood carbon dioxide directly drives the bicarbonate buffer equation,

27
00:01:27.255 --> 00:01:31.068
so the same change happens to the hydrogen ion level.

28
00:01:31.068 --> 00:01:34.522
And a change in hydrogen ions is a change in pH.

29
00:01:34.972 --> 00:01:39.524
So in Manaia's hyperventilation, she blows off carbon dioxide.

30
00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:44.149
The buffer uses up hydrogen ions and bicarbonate to replace it.

31
00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:46.792
Hydrogen ions fall, so the pH rises.

32
00:01:46.792 --> 00:01:48.921
That's respiratory alkalosis.

33
00:01:48.921 --> 00:01:55.308
Hypoventilation, after a head injury for example, does the opposite: carbon dioxide up,

34
00:01:55.308 --> 00:01:57.217
hydrogen ions up, pH down.

35
00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.759
Respiratory acidosis.

36
00:02:00.209 --> 00:02:00.928
<v Examiner>Two marks.

37
00:02:00.928 --> 00:02:07.047
<v Examiner>How does the respiratory system respond when something causes the blood pH to change?

38
00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:09.638
<v Examiner>Think back to ketoacidosis in B N 1.

39
00:02:10.088 --> 00:02:15.229
Now it's the other way round: the pH problem comes first, and breathing fixes it.

40
00:02:15.679 --> 00:02:20.458
Metabolic acidosis, with hydrogen ions up, like in ketoacidosis.

41
00:02:20.458 --> 00:02:23.445
The body responds with hyperventilation.

42
00:02:23.445 --> 00:02:27.775
Hydrogen ions are converted into carbon dioxide and water,

43
00:02:27.775 --> 00:02:31.135
and the extra carbon dioxide is breathed off.

44
00:02:31.135 --> 00:02:33.301
The breathing rate increases.

45
00:02:33.751 --> 00:02:36.969
Metabolic alkalosis, with hydrogen ions down.

46
00:02:36.969 --> 00:02:39.758
The body responds with hypoventilation.

47
00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:45.622
Carbon dioxide is kept in the body, and turned into hydrogen ions and bicarbonate,

48
00:02:45.622 --> 00:02:48.125
to replace the ones that were lost.

49
00:02:48.125 --> 00:02:50.199
The breathing rate decreases.

50
00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:52.760
The same buffer works both ways.

51
00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:54.410
Too acid, breathe faster.

52
00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:56.323
Too alkaline, breathe slower.

53
00:02:56.773 --> 00:02:57.446
<v Examiner>Once more.

54
00:02:57.446 --> 00:02:58.992
<v Examiner>Breathing and blood pH.

55
00:03:01.442 --> 00:03:06.324
Normal blood pH is seven point three five to seven point four five.

56
00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:12.519
The buffer: carbon dioxide plus water, carbonic acid, hydrogen ions plus bicarbonate,

57
00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:13.247
both ways.

58
00:03:13.247 --> 00:03:19.806
Breathing sets the blood's carbon dioxide, and hydrogen ions follow it, so pH follows too.

59
00:03:19.806 --> 00:03:26.000
Manaia's hyperventilation blows off carbon dioxide, hydrogen ions fall, and pH rises:

60
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.603
respiratory alkalosis.

61
00:03:27.603 --> 00:03:31.466
Hypoventilation is the reverse: respiratory acidosis.

62
00:03:31.466 --> 00:03:37.878
In metabolic acidosis the body hyperventilates, to breathe off the extra carbon dioxide;

63
00:03:37.878 --> 00:03:41.741
in metabolic alkalosis it hypoventilates, to keep it.
